--- body ---
## Question
Should `Supersedes: NNNN` become a first-class %erg header (with
cross-ref validation in erg check, like Blocked-by)? Today it is
rejected as an unknown header. Known prior: exactly ONE wild instance
(aedist 0292 superseding 0291, found during the 0407 migration and
folded into the log).

The STATE bar applies: "verified empirical need" -- no feature without
evidence. This audit produces the evidence, or kills the idea.

## Decision criteria -- FIXED BEFORE the sweep (do not adjust after)
A "supersession event" = one ticket whose log, body, or Closed: reason
declares that another, NAMED ticket replaces it or is replaced by it
(naming an ID is required; vague "this is obsolete" does not count).

- ADOPT   : >= 10 events across >= 3 stores. Cross-project recurrence
            proves the relation wants tooling support.
- REJECT  : < 4 events after a full sweep, OR futility stop (below).
            Close this ticket with the counts; the log-line convention
            (see 0292) remains the documented way.
- DEFER   : 4-9 events. Keep the log convention, re-audit on the next
            machine-wide migration or in ~3 months, whichever first.

## Early-stopping rules (sequential, sweep largest stores first)
Sweep order, descending corpus size (machine padme, 2026-06-04):
aedist (391), chemin-de-voix (225), git-erg (220), IDH (197),
Climate_finance (126), fuzzy-corpus (51), cadens (37), padme (13).

- STOP-ADOPT  : the moment the running tally reaches 10 events across
                >= 3 stores -- stop sweeping, decide ADOPT.
- STOP-REJECT : after the three largest stores (836 tickets, ~66% of
                the ~1260 corpus) yield <= 1 event total -- stop, decide
                REJECT. Rationale: 2.5x the remaining corpus could not
                reach the DEFER floor at the observed density.

## Sweep mechanics
Per store, candidate grep (case-insensitive, EN+FR stores):
  grep -rliE 'supersed|replaced by|obsoleted by|superseded|remplac[ée]
  par|voir plut[oô]t' tickets/ --include='*.erg'
Then human-verify each hit: count it only if it NAMES a ticket ID and
expresses replacement (not mere mention). Record per-store counts and
the verified event list in this ticket's log as the sweep proceeds --
the running log IS the early-stop state.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Criteria above untouched since filing (audit integrity)
- [ ] Sweep log: per-store verified counts, in the declared order,
      stopping where a rule fires
- [ ] Decision recorded: ADOPT / REJECT / DEFER with the tally
- [ ] If ADOPT: spec ticket spawned (header semantics, check rules,
      migrate rule for retrofitting log-line conventions); this ticket
      closes referencing it. Otherwise this ticket closes with the
      verdict.
